  "reasoning": "The patient presents with severe chills ('shivering and shivering') and a high fever, which are indicative of an acute systemic infection. The key differentiating symptom is the presence of rashes on the arms and red patches on the neck. While chills and high fever are hallmark signs of malaria, the distinct rash distribution strongly points to dengue fever, which classically presents with a maculopapular rash on the extremities and neck following the onset of high fever and rigors. Conditions like chicken pox typically feature widespread itchy vesicles, and drug reactions generally lack such pronounced rigors. Thus, the clinical picture best aligns with dengue.",
  "final_answer": "[DIAGNOSIS]dengue[/DIAGNOSIS]"
}
